`res.send()` recognizes DataView as binary via `ArrayBuffer.isView()`, but later calls `Buffer.from(dataView)`, which produces an empty Buffer. A non-empty view therefore returns HTTP 200 with `Content-Length: 0`.

Reproduced with Express 5.2.1, current master (`9efc29e`), Node 24.14.1, Linux:

```js
const express = require('express')
const app = express()
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  const bytes = new Uint8Array([0, 1, 2, 3, 0])
  res.send(new DataView(bytes.buffer, 1, 3))
})
app.listen(3000)
// GET / returns an empty response instead of the bytes 01 02 03.
```

Expected: send exactly the DataView's byte range, with the correct Content-Length and ETag. The bug also occurs with ETags disabled and with cross-realm views.

This revisits the DataView-specific problem from #7433, which its author closed without merging. It is separate from bare ArrayBuffer support in #7362.

A focused patch is ready: detect DataViews with `node:util.types.isDataView`, then convert only their backing byte range. Buffer and other typed-array behavior remain unchanged, including the Uint16Array compatibility concern raised in #7433.

The patch adds 11 regression cases (eight fail before the fix); all 1,272 tests pass with `npm run test-ci`, and `npm run lint` passes. No dependency changes.

AI assistance was used for investigation, implementation, and local validation.

This is a minimal, runnable reproduction for issue #7427. 

When an `express()` sub-app is mounted via `router.use()` instead of `app.use()`, the `req` and `res` prototypes are swapped by `app.handle` but never restored when the sub-app calls `next()`. 

As a result, any middleware that runs *after* the sub-app finishes will silently inherit the sub-app's configuration (like `trust proxy`) instead of the parent app's configuration.

### Expected Behavior
After a sub-app mounted on a router calls `next()`, the `req` prototype should be restored so subsequent middleware uses the parent app's settings.

### Reproduction Script
```js
const express = require('express');

const app = express();
const router = express.Router();
const subApp = express();

// Parent app does NOT trust proxy
app.set('trust proxy', false);

// Sub app DOES trust proxy
subApp.set('trust proxy', true);
subApp.use((req, res, next) => {
  // Do nothing, just pass back to the parent router
  next(); 
});

// BUG TRIGGER: Mounting subApp via router instead of app.use
router.use('/test', subApp);
app.use(router);

app.use('/test', (req, res) => {
  // Expected: false (Parent app setting)
  // Actual: true (Leaked sub-app setting!)
  res.json({ 
    isProxyTrusted: req.app.get('trust proxy') 
  });
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Listening on 3000. Run: curl http://localhost:3000/test');
});



System Info
Express version: 4.21.2
Node.js version: 20.x
OS: MacOS

When an express() sub-app is mounted via router.use() instead of app.use(), the request and response prototypes are swapped by app.handle but never restored. The sub-app also never fires the mount event, so it does not inherit the parent's trust proxy setting. Any middleware that runs after the sub-app calls next() reads req.ip, req.secure, and req.hostname under the wrong app's trust policy, silently and without any error.

app.handle (lib/application.js:169-170) performs:

js
Object.setPrototypeOf(req, this.request)
Object.setPrototypeOf(res, this.response)

The only code that reverses this swap is the mounted_app closure built by app.use at lib/application.js:230-237. router.use never builds that closure, so the swap is permanent for the lifetime of the request.

The router already saves and restores request state across boundaries at index.js:171:

js
let done = restore(callback, req, 'baseUrl', 'next', 'params')

The prototype is simply not on the list.

Add __proto__ (or Object.getPrototypeOf equivalents) to the restore call so the router's own cleanup undoes the prototype swap on every exit path — next(), next(err), and unhandled throws alike.

js
// index.js — inside Router.prototype.handle, before the loop
var reqProto = Object.getPrototypeOf(req)
var resProto = Object.getPrototypeOf(res)

var done = restore(callback, req, 'baseUrl', 'next', 'params')
var _done = done
done = function(err) {
    Object.setPrototypeOf(req, reqProto)
    Object.setPrototypeOf(res, resProto)
    _done(err)
}

